# Performance

Do not prematurely optimize. Apply performance techniques only when there is a measurable or clearly anticipated problem.

## Re-renders

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders by keeping state as close to where it is used as possible.
- Do not lift state higher than needed — unnecessary global or shared state causes cascading re-renders.
- Split components so that only the part that changes re-renders, not the entire parent.

## Memoization

- Use `useMemo` and `useCallback` only when the computation is expensive or when referential equality matters (e.g. props passed to memoized children).
- Do not wrap every value or function in `useMemo`/`useCallback` by default — it adds overhead and reduces readability.
- Use `React.memo` on components that receive stable props but re-render frequently due to parent updates.

## Lazy Loading

- Lazy load page-level components using `React.lazy` and `Suspense`.
- Do not lazy load small or shared components — the overhead is not worth it.

```tsx
const TransactionsPage = React.lazy(() => import('./pages/TransactionsPage'));
```

## General Rules

- Prefer derived values over redundant state — compute from existing state instead of storing a copy.
- Avoid anonymous functions and object literals in JSX props when they cause unnecessary re-renders in sensitive components.
- Profile before optimizing — do not assume a bottleneck exists without evidence.
